1;Facilitate IPM
1;Provide health education
1;Refer to another SPFA
1;Refer to the Primary Care Physician (PCP) communicating the PRM/RNM
1;Refer to the primary care physician (PCP) proposing changes in treatment
1;Propose other modifications
1;Notify pharmacovigilance in accordance with current legislation
2;Medication error;Use of a route and/or form of administration of the medication other than the one indicated. This includes the type of route and pharmaceutical form, method of administration (before, after…, with or without food, use of an otic form orally, orally, enterally or orally or orodispersibly…).;1st Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to the administration of medications by the patient/caregiver, professional or not
2;Inadequate preservation;Storage carried out outside of the recommendations or specifications described in the technical data sheet (temperature, humidity conditions, etc.);2nd Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to the use of medications by the patient/caregiver
2;Inadequate dosage, regimen, and/or duration;Mistake regarding the regimen, dosage, and/or duration of a medication as prescribed, indicated, etc. Abuse;2nd Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to the use of medications by the patient/caregiver
2;Errors in medication use;Mistake in the correct use of the medication. This includes improper preparation (untimely discontinuation), misuse (cutting a patch), use of an expired medication, or use of a medication that requires skill, handling, or dexterity.;2nd Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to the use of medications by the patient/caregiver
2;Lack of knowledge about how to use the medication;Lack of knowledge regarding the correct use of medication: what it is for, how, how much, or when to administer it. This does not apply to initial treatments.;2nd Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to the use of medications by the patient/caregiver
2;Does not take medication/lack of adherence;Lack of agreement between the recommendations agreed upon by the healthcare professional and the patient regarding the proper process for using medications;2nd Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to the use of medications by the patient/caregiver
2;High probability of adverse effects;The medicine (active ingredient/excipient) may cause an unwanted effect at the doses defined in the product information leaflet.;3rd Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to adverse drug effects
2;Interactions with other medications, medicinal plants, food supplements or foods;A reaction between two or more medications, or between a medication and a food, dietary supplement, or medicinal plant, that can alter the effect of at least one medication. It may decrease its effectiveness, increase toxicity or safety, or cause unexpected effects.;3rd Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to adverse drug effects
2;Personal characteristics;Patient characteristics, whether due to personal beliefs, physiological condition, etc., that pose a problem for the proper use of medication;4th Dimension. PRM related to the patient's personal and physiological characteristics
2;Contraindications;A physiological or pathological condition in which a particular medication should not be used because it may be harmful. These are detailed in the product information leaflet.;4th Dimension. PRM related to the patient's personal and physiological characteristics
2;Caution for use;A physiological or pathological situation in which a particular medication must be monitored to prevent harm.;4th Dimension. PRM related to the patient's personal and physiological characteristics
2;Dispensing errors;Error on the part of the pharmacist or under their responsibility in the act of dispensing a medication;5th Dimension. PRM related to the healthcare professional
2;Errors in prescription or indication;An error on the part of the healthcare professional in prescribing a medication. It is not considered a drug-related problem (DRP) when the physician voluntarily prescribes a medication outside of its authorized use.;5th Dimension. PRM related to the healthcare professional
2;Lack of reconciliation between levels;Discrepancies regarding patient medication are not identified due to a lack of reconciliation between different levels of care.;5th Dimension. PRM related to the healthcare professional
2;Duplicity;Detection, in a pharmacotherapeutic plan, of two medications with the same active ingredient or mechanism of action without being intentional by the prescriber(s);5th Dimension. PRM related to the healthcare professional
2;Unnecessary medication;The patient uses a medication without clinical justification or a justified therapeutic purpose. It is not considered a drug-related problem (DRP) when the physician voluntarily prescribes a medication outside of its authorized use.;5th Dimension. PRM related to the healthcare professional
2;Undertreated Health Problems (HP);Deficient or non-existent therapeutic strategy to achieve control of a PS;6th Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to expected drug outcomes
2;Other PS or special physiological situations that affect treatment;A state of health or special physiological condition that may determine the need for, effectiveness and/or safety of a pharmacological treatment and, therefore, the control of the disease;6th Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to expected drug outcomes
2;Genetic polymorphism;An individual genotype that affects the response to drug treatment in terms of effectiveness and safety. It is not considered a drug-related problem (DRP) when a physician voluntarily prescribes a medication outside of its authorized use.;6th Dimension. Drug-related problems (DRPs) related to expected drug outcomes
2;Temporary supply problem (shortage);Irregular supply of a medication that hinders access to treatment;7th Dimension. PRM related to the healthcare system
2;Problem related to access to medication;The patient needs treatment, the doctor prescribes it, but for various socio-economic, employment, administrative, etc. reasons, the recipient of the medication cannot access it.;7th Dimension. PRM related to the healthcare system
2;Others;Any circumstance other than those described above that may cause MRI;8th Dimension. Other PRM
